INSERT INTO public.blog_posts (id, title, slug, excerpt, content, image, category, author, author_avatar, read_time, views, likes, tags, content_blocks, status, created_at)
VALUES (
  6,
  'Why Tajweed is Important When Learning the Quran',
  'why-tajweed-is-important-when-learning-the-quran',
  'Why Tajweed is Important When Learning the Quran When Muslims recite the Kalam Allah, they are not just reading words. They are repeating the exact speech that Allah revealed to th',
  '<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-size:23.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Why Tajweed is Important
When Learning the Quran</span></strong><strong><span style="font-size:24.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></strong></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">When Muslims recite the
Kalam Allah, they are not just reading words. They are repeating the exact
speech that Allah revealed to the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) through
Jibreel (peace be upon him).</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">That speech came with
specific sounds. Specific rhythms. Specific rules governing every letter and
every breath.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Those rules are Tajweed.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Understanding why
Tajweed matters is not an academic exercise. It changes how a student
approaches every single recitation. And it explains why every serious scholar
throughout Islamic history has treated Tajweed not as optional but as essential.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:center; line-height:normal"><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif">



</span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-size:17.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">What Tajweed Actually Means</span></strong><strong><span style="font-size:18.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></strong></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">The word &#39;Tajweed&#39; comes
from the Arabic root meaning &#39;to improve&#39; or &#39;to make better&#39;. In the context
of Quran recitation, it refers to the complete set of rules governing how each
letter is pronounced, how long vowels are elongated, where the voice stops, and
how one letter affects the sound of the next.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">These rules were not
invented by scholars. They were extracted and documented from the way the
Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) recited. Every rule traces back to
authentic chains of transmission reaching back fourteen centuries.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">When a student decides
to <strong>learn Quran with Tajweed</strong>, they are not learning a scholarly addition.
They are learning the original way the Sacred Text was meant to be recited.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:center; line-height:normal"><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif">



</span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-size:17.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">What Happens Without Tajweed</span></strong><strong><span style="font-size:18.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></strong></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Arabic is an
exceptionally precise language. Small changes in pronunciation can change
meaning entirely.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">The letter &#39;qaaf&#39; and
the letter &#39;kaaf&#39; look similar to a beginner. But they come from completely
different points in the throat and mouth. Confusing them changes words.
Changing words changes meaning. In the context of the Kalam Kareem, changing
meaning is a serious matter.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Imam Ibn Al-Jazari,
whose authority on Tajweed is unrivalled in Islamic scholarship, stated,
&quot;Applying Tajweed to the Quran is an obligation. Whoever does not apply it
is a sinner.&quot;</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">This is not an extreme
position. It reflects the weight Muslims have always placed on preserving the
integrity of the Sacred Text exactly as it was revealed.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Poor Tajweed habits
formed early are also notoriously difficult to correct later. A student who
spends two years reciting with incorrect pronunciation often needs another full
year just to undo those habits. Starting correctly from the beginning is not
just better. It is more efficient.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:center; line-height:normal"><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif">



</span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-size:17.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">How Tajweed Deepens Spiritual Connection</span></strong><strong><span style="font-size:18.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></strong></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Correct recitation is
not only about accuracy. It produces a different experience of the Sacred Text
altogether.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">When each letter is
given its proper sound, when elongations are applied in the right places, when
the voice pauses where it should and continues where it should, the recitation
takes on a quality that moves the heart.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Allah says in Al-Kitab:
&quot;And recite the Quran with measured recitation.&quot; (Surah Al-Muzzammil,
73:4) The Arabic word used here is &#39;Tarteel&#39;. It means slow, clear, and
beautiful recitation. Tajweed is the science that makes Tarteel possible.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Many students report
that their relationship with the Kalam Allah changed completely once they began
applying Tajweed properly. The recitation slowed down. The words became more
deliberate. The meaning became more present. The act of reading became an act
of worship in the fullest sense.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">This is what <strong>Quran
recitation tajweed</strong> produces. Not just correct reading. A deeper, more conscious
connection to what is being said.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:center; line-height:normal"><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif">



</span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-size:17.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">How Tajweed Classes Online Make This Accessible</span></strong><strong><span style="font-size:18.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></strong></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">For much of Islamic
history, learning Tajweed required physical proximity to a qualified scholar.
That limited access for Muslims living in the West or in areas with small
Muslim communities.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Tajweed classes online</span></strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black"> have changed this entirely.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Today, a student in
London, Toronto, Sydney, or Houston can connect with a scholar trained in the
great Islamic institutions of Egypt, Saudi Arabia, or Pakistan. The lesson
happens via a secure video call. The teacher listens. The student recites.
Corrections happen in real time.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">The quality of this
instruction is identical to in-person learning. What changes is accessibility.
Geography is no longer a barrier. Schedule rigidity is no longer a barrier. Any
student with genuine commitment can now access genuine Tajweed education.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Tajweed Quran online</span></strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black"> platforms that prioritise qualified teachers and structured
curricula have made this standard of learning available to families worldwide.
The question is no longer whether good Tajweed instruction exists. It is
whether the student will commit to it.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:center; line-height:normal"><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif">



</span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-size:17.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">The Role of a Qualified Teacher in Tajweed
Learning</span></strong><strong><span style="font-size:18.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></strong></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Tajweed cannot be
learned from books or videos alone. This point cannot be overstated.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Books explain rules.
Videos demonstrate sounds. But only a qualified teacher can listen to your
specific recitation, identify your specific errors, and correct them in real
time with specific guidance tailored to your voice and your habits.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">This is why the
tradition of Tajweed has always been oral. Teacher to student. Mouth to ear.
Correction to improvement.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">When choosing from <strong>Quran
teachers online</strong> for Tajweed instruction, look for teachers who hold an
Ijazah. An Ijazah confirms that the teacher has themselves completed the full
Quran with correct Tajweed under a qualified scholar and received formal
certification. Their chain of transmission is authentic and documented.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Also look for patience
and the ability to explain the same concept multiple ways. Different students
understand through different approaches. A great Tajweed teacher finds the
explanation that works for each student, not the one that works for most.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">As we covered in detail
in our guide on</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"><a href="https://ajwaacademy.com/blog/learn-quran-tajweed-online" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><span style="font-size:11.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black"> </span><span style="font-size:11.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#1155CC">the Step-by-Step Guide to Learn
Quran with Tajweed Online</span></a></span><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">, the quality of the
teacher shapes the student&#39;s recitation for life. This choice deserves serious
attention.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:center; line-height:normal"><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif">



</span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-size:17.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Benefits of Joining a Tajweed Course Online</span></strong><strong><span style="font-size:18.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></strong></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">A structured <strong>tajweed
course online</strong> brings several advantages that self-study simply cannot
provide.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Clear progression.</span></strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black"> Each stage builds on the previous one. Makharij first. Then
Sifaat. Then the rules of Noon Sakinah and Tanween. Then Madd. Then Qalqala and
Waqf. Every rule is introduced at the right time, not before the student is
ready.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Flexible scheduling.</span></strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black"> Families across the UK, USA, Canada, Australia, and UAE can fit
lessons around school, work, and family commitments. Morning slots, evening
slots, and weekends are all available. Consistency becomes manageable because
the schedule serves the student.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Regular assessment.</span></strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black"> A good programme tracks progress honestly. Parents receive
detailed monthly reports. Adult students know exactly which rules they have
mastered and which need more work. This transparency keeps motivation high and
direction clear.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Female teachers
available.</span></strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black"> Many families require female scholars for
daughters and sisters. A reputable <strong>online tajweed course</strong> provider offers
this without making it an exception.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Free trial lesson.</span></strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black"> Any serious provider allows a trial session before commitment.
This gives the student and family a genuine opportunity to assess the teacher&#39;s
style, patience, and communication before enrolling.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:center; line-height:normal"><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif">



</span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-size:17.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Practical Tips for Consistent Tajweed Practice
at Home</span></strong><strong><span style="font-size:18.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></strong></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Lessons provide
instruction. Daily home practice builds the skill.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Here are habits that
accelerate Tajweed progress between sessions.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Recite slowly every day.
Speed is the enemy of Tajweed. A student who reads slowly and correctly for ten
minutes learns more than one who rushes through two pages.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Focus on one rule at a
time. After each lesson, identify the rule that was most recently introduced.
Spend three days applying only that rule in your home recitation before adding
the next one.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Record your recitation
regularly. Play it back and listen critically. You will catch mistakes that
felt invisible while reciting. Bring specific examples to your next lesson.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Listen to qualified
reciters daily. Sheikh Mahmoud Khalil Al-Husary is widely regarded as the gold
standard for Tajweed recitation. Listening to his Mujawwad recitation trains
the ear in ways that practice alone cannot replicate.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Keep a Tajweed notebook.
Write each rule in your own words after the lesson. This reinforces
understanding and creates a personal reference you can review between sessions.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Make dua before every
session and every practice. Ask Allah to make the Kalam Allah easy for you.
This intention transforms learning from an educational task into an act of
worship.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:center; line-height:normal"><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif">



</span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-size:17.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Who Should Learn Quran with Tajweed</span></strong><strong><span style="font-size:18.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></strong></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">The honest answer is
every Muslim who recites the Sacred Text.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Children benefit most
from starting Tajweed early. Correct habits formed in childhood stay for life.
The brain absorbs sounds more naturally at a young age. A child who learns
proper Makharij before the age of ten will likely carry that precision into adulthood
without effort.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Adult beginners benefit
from the structure and accountability that a formal programme provides. Many
adults who begin <strong>tajweed classes online</strong> as complete beginners progress
faster than they expected. Their motivation is stronger. Their understanding is
deeper. Their commitment to practice is more consistent.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Even those who already
read the Sacred Text often discover after their first proper Tajweed lesson
that they were making systematic errors they had never noticed. Correcting
those errors transforms their recitation in ways that feel immediately
significant.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">As we discussed in our
article on</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"><a href="https://ajwaacademy.com/blog/learn-quran-tajweed-home" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><span style="font-size:11.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black"> </span><span style="font-size:11.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#1155CC">How to Learn Quran Online with
Tajweed at Home</span></a></span><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">, the journey to correct
recitation is available to every student at every level. The starting point is
simply honesty about where you are now.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:center; line-height:normal"><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif">



</span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-size:17.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Building Confidence Through Tajweed</span></strong><strong><span style="font-size:18.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></strong></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Many students hesitate
to recite in front of others because they are not confident in their
pronunciation. This hesitation is understandable. But it is also reversible.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Every student who
commits to <strong>learn Quran with Tajweed</strong> under qualified guidance reports the
same experience. Confidence grows with each corrected mistake. The voice
becomes steadier. The recitation becomes more assured. What once felt uncertain
becomes natural.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">This confidence extends
beyond recitation. It changes how students relate to the Sacred Text in prayer,
in reflection, and in daily life. The Kalam of Allah becomes something they
carry with genuine ownership, not anxiety.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">That transformation
begins with a single decision. To learn it properly. To give the Sacred Text
the effort it deserves.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:center; line-height:normal"><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif">



</span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-size:17.0pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Conclusion</span></strong><strong><span style="font-size:18.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></strong></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Tajweed is not a detail.
It is the foundation of every correct recitation of the Kalam Allah.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Learn the Quran with
Tajweed</span></strong><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black"> under a qualified teacher. Join a structured <strong>Tajweed
course online</strong> that follows a clear progression. Practise daily at home with
focus and patience. And trust that every correctly applied rule brings you
closer to the standard of recitation that the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon
him) demonstrated and taught.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">The tools are available today
that were never available to previous generations of Western Muslims. Qualified
scholars. Flexible schedules. Real-time correction from anywhere in the world.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">There is no barrier left
except the decision to begin.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left; line-height:normal"><span style="font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:black">Make that decision
today. The sacred text is waiting to be recited the way it deserves.</span><span style="font-size:12.0pt; font-family:Times New Roman,serif"></span></p>

<p style="text-align:left"> </p>',
