VALUES (
  31,
  'Menstruation in Islam: A Complete Hanafi Guide for Women',
  'menstruation-in-islam-a-complete-hanafi-guide-for-women',
  'Menstruation in Islam: A Complete Hanafi Guide for Women Introduction Many Muslim women have questions about menstruation that nobody answers clearly. New Muslims especially find i',
  '<h2 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:17pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Menstruation in Islam: A Complete Hanafi Guide for Women</span></h2><h3 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:13pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Introduction</span></h3><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Many Muslim women have questions about menstruation that nobody answers clearly. New Muslims especially find it confusing. Even born Muslims. Sometimes they are not sure about the rules.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">This guide explains everything simply and clearly. It follows the Hanafi school of thought, which is the most widely followed madhhab among Muslims in the UK, USA, Canada, Australia, and the UAE.</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"></p><h3 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:13pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">What Is Menstruation (Haidh) in Islam?</span></h3><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">In Islam, menstruation is called </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">&#39;haidh.&#39;</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> It is a natural monthly cycle that Allah has created for women.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">It is not something shameful or dirty. It is simply a state that carries specific Islamic rulings. Understanding these rulings helps a Muslim woman worship Allah correctly throughout her life.</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"></p><h3 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:13pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">How Many Days Is a Period in Islam?</span></h3><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">This is one of the most searched questions among Muslim women worldwide.</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0)">Minimum days:</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0)"> 3 days</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">Maximum days:</span><span style="font-size:11pt"> 10 days</span></p> </span><p></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">If blood comes for less than 3 days, it is </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">not Haidh</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">. It is called </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">&quot;istihadha,&quot;</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> which means irregular bleeding due to illness or another cause.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">If bleeding continues beyond 10 days, the extra days are also </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">not haidh.</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> They are Istihadha as well.</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"></p><h3 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:13pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">What Are the Colors of Menstrual Blood?</span></h3><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Many women ask about colors. This is important because the color tells you whether your period has started or ended.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">According to Hanafi Fiqh, during the days of Haidh, </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">any discharge in these six colors is considered Haidh:</span></p><ul><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Red</span><span style="font-size:11pt"> (سرخ)</span></p></li><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Yellow</span><span style="font-size:11pt"> (زرد)</span></p></li><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Green</span><span style="font-size:11pt"> (سبز)</span></p></li><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Dust-colored</span><span style="font-size:11pt"> (مٹیالا)</span></p></li><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Black</span><span style="font-size:11pt"> (سیاہ)</span></p></li><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Muddy/Dark</span><span style="font-size:11pt"> (گدلا)</span></p></li></ul><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Pure white discharge is NOT haidh.</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> When a woman sees pure white discharge or a completely clean pad, her period has ended.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">This ruling is mentioned in Al-Mabsoot by Imam Sarakhsi, one of the most authoritative texts in Hanafi Fiqh.</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"></p><h3 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:13pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Three Types of Women Regarding Their Period</span></h3><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Women fall into three categories when it comes to haidh:</span></p><h4 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Type 1: Woman Who Knows Her Habit</span></h4><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">She knows her period comes for exactly 5 or 6 days every month. This is her established habit.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">If, one month, her bleeding continues for 7 or 8 days and stops before 10 days, her habit has changed. The new number of days becomes her new habit.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">But if bleeding continues beyond 10 days, only her original habit days count as Haidh. The extra days are istihadha.</span></p><h4 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Type 2: Woman Who Does Not Know Her Habit</span></h4><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">She has no established pattern. Whatever days the bleeding comes, those are her Haidh days, as long as they fall between 3 and 10 days in length.</span></p><h4 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Type 3: Woman Who Has Never Had a Period Before</span></h4><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">She will count 3 days as Haidh for the first time. After that, whatever pattern develops becomes her habit.</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"></p><h3 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:13pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">What About Spotting?</span></h3><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">This confuses many women. The answer is clear in Hanafi Fiqh.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">If spotting occurs during your normal period days and</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> the total days, including spotting, do not exceed 10 days, then </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">the spotting is part of haidh.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">If spotting occurs after your period has ended and</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> your pad or cloth shows any color other than pure white, you are still in haidh until it becomes completely clean.</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:11pt">As stated in Fatawa Shami:</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">&quot;If the days do not exceed the maximum, it is considered a change of habit and counts as Haidh.&quot;</span></p> </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">(Kitab al-Taharah, Bab al-Haidh, 1/385)</span></p></span><p></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"></p><h3 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:13pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">What Is Not Allowed During Haidh?</span></h3><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">During her period, a woman must avoid the following:</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:11pt">Salah (Prayer):</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">She does not pray during haidh. Unlike missed fasts, </span><span style="font-size:11pt">missed prayers do NOT need to be made up (qadha).</span><span style="font-size:11pt"> This is a mercy from Allah.</span></p></span><p></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:11pt">Fasting:</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">She does not fast during haidh. However, </span><span style="font-size:11pt">missed fasts must be made up</span><span style="font-size:11pt"> after her period ends.</span></p></span><p></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Reciting the Quran, she</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> cannot recite the Kalam Allah as continuous recitation during Haidh. However, if she is a student and must read for learning purposes, she should pause between words so it does not count as full recitation.</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:11pt">Entering the Masjid:</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">She cannot enter the mosque during her period.</span></p></span><p></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:11pt">Marital Relations</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">Physical intimacy with her husband is not permitted during haidh.</span></p></span><p></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"></p><h3 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:13pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">What About the Gap Between Two Periods?</span></h3><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">This is an important ruling that many women do not know.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">There must be at least 15 days of purity between two periods.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">If bleeding returns before 15 days of purity have passed, that new bleeding is </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Istihadha</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">, not a new haidh.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">During Istihadha, the woman is considered pure. She must pray, fast, and recite the Kalam Pak. She makes a fresh wudhu for each prayer time if bleeding is continuous.</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:11pt">This ruling is confirmed in Al-Bahr al-Ra&#39;iq:</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">&quot;Purity of less than fifteen days between two bleedings does not separate them. They are treated as continuous bleeding.&quot;</span></p> </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">(</span><a href="https://darulfiqh.com/what-is-the-ruling-of-bleeding-again-before-15-days-of-purity-elapse/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" style="font-family:Georgia, Times New Roman, serif; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#1155cc">Kitab al-Taharah, Bab al-Haidh, 1/216</span></a><span style="font-size:11pt">)</span></p></span><p></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"></p><h3 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:13pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">How Does a Woman Know Her Period Has Ended?</span></h3><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Her period has ended when</span></p><ul><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Her pad or cloth becomes </span><span style="font-size:11pt">completely white and clean</span></p></li><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">OR she sees </span><span style="font-size:11pt">pure white discharge</span></p></li></ul><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">If she still sees any yellow, brown, or other colored discharge during her habitual days, she is still in Haidh.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">What if she sees yellow discharge after her habit days are over?</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">If the yellow discharge appears after her normal habit days but before 10 total days have passed, it is still Haidh. Her habit has changed to include those extra days.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">If it appears after 10 days, it is Istihadha.</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"></p><h3 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:13pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">What to Do When Period Ends</span></h3><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">When a woman is certain her period has ended, she must perform </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Ghusl</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> (full ritual bath).</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Requirements of Ghusl:</span></p><ul><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Rinse the mouth thoroughly (Madhmadhah)</span></p></li><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Put water into the nose (Istinshaq)</span></p></li><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Wash the entire body</span></p></li></ul><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">These two steps, rinsing the mouth and putting water in the nose, are </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Fardh</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> in Ghusl al-Janabah and Ghusl al-Haidh. If they are missed, the Ghusl is not complete, and prayer is not valid.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">After valid Ghusl, </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">separate Wudu is not needed.</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> The Ghusl itself includes Wudhu. This is confirmed in Tirmidhi Sharif:</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:11pt">Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) reported that the Prophet (peace be upon him) did not make Wudhu after Ghusl.</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">(Abwab al-Taharah, 1/30)</span></p></span><p></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">However, if something that breaks Wudhu occurs after Ghusl, a new Wudhu must be made before prayer.</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"></p><h3 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:13pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">FAQ Section</span></h3><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:11pt">Q: Is brown discharge considered a period in Islam?</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">A: Yes. Brown discharge during your period days counts as Haidh according to Hanafi Fiqh, as confirmed in Fatawa Shami.</span></p></span><p></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:11pt">Q: What if my period goes over 10 days?</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">A: Only your established habit days count as Haidh. The remaining days are Istihadha. You perform Ghusl after your habit days and resume prayer.</span></p></span><p></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:11pt">Q: Do I make up missed prayers from my period?</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">A: No. Missed prayers during Haidh do not need to be made up. This is a specific mercy from Allah for women.</span></p></span><p></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:11pt">Q: Do I make up missed fasts from my period?</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">A: Yes. Missed fasts must be made up after your period ends, before the next Ramadan.</span></p></span><p></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:11pt">Q: Can I recite the Quran during my period?</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">A: Continuous recitation is not permitted. If necessary for learning, pause between words so it does not count as full recitation.</span></p></span><p></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:11pt">Q: When can I pray again after my period?</span></p><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:11pt">A: Once your pad or cloth is completely clean and white, perform ghusl, and you may pray.</span></p></span><p></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"></p><h3 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:13pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">A Note for New Muslim Women</span></h3><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">If you are new to Islam, these rulings may feel overwhelming at first. That is completely normal.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">The most important thing to remember is this. Islam has made these rules with wisdom and mercy. Missing prayer and fasting during your period is not a sin. It is what Allah has prescribed for you.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">If you have specific questions about your situation, our qualified </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">female Islamic teachers</span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> at Ajwa Academy are available to guide you privately and respectfully. Learn more about our</span><a href="https://www.ajwaacademy.com/blog/best-online-quran-classes-for-sisters-and-women" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#1155cc">online Quran classes for sisters</span></a><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> and how our female scholars can support your Islamic learning journey.</span></p><p style="line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"></p><p style="font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"></p><h3 style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:13pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Conclusion</span></h3><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">Understanding menstruation in Islam is not complicated when it is explained clearly. The key points are:</span></p><ul><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Minimum 3 days, maximum 10 days</span></p></li><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Six colors count as Haidh; pure white does not</span></p></li><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">At least 15 days of purity required between two periods</span></p></li><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Prayer is not made up; fasts must be made up</span></p></li><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Ghusl is required when period ends</span></p></li><li style="font-size:1.35rem; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; color:rgb(0, 0, 0); line-height:1.7"><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt">Mouth rinsing and nose washing are Fardh in Ghusl</span></p></li></ul><p style="text-align:left; line-height:1.7; font-size:1.35rem"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">For more guidance on Islamic rulings and Quran learning, explore our</span><a href="https://www.ajwaacademy.com/blog/benefits-of-learning-quran-online-at-home-1777341886083" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#1155cc">complete guide to online Quran learning</span></a><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> and our</span><a href="https://www.ajwaacademy.com/blog/best-online-quran-classes-for-beginners" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000"> </span><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#1155cc">online Quran classes for beginners</span></a><span style="font-size:11pt; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; color:#000000">.</span></p><p style="text-align:left; font-size:1.35rem; line-height:1.7"><span style="font-size:21.6px"><br /></span></p>',
  NULL,
  'Women''s Islamic Guide',
  'Muhammed Ibrahim ',
  NULL,
  '5 min read',
  0,
  0,
